===Noun===
{{en-noun|s}}

# A particular [[period]] of [[history]], or of a person's [[life]], ''especially'' one considered [[noteworthy]] or [[remarkable]].
#: {{syn|en|age|era|q2=only in general usage}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=198|volume=I|text=I grew bitter in my words—I believed the worst of everyone; nay, I sometimes doubted the affection of my kind, my indulgent parents. But let me hastily pass over this vain and profitless '''epoch''',—the fierce tempest, and the weary calm, were but the appointed means by which I reached the harbour of faith and rest.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=s:en:The Waning of the Middle Ages|year=1924|tlr=F. Hopman|by=w:Johan Huizinga|original=Herfsttij der Middeleeuwen|passage=And it occasionally happens that a period in which one had, hitherto, been mainly looking for the coming to birth of new things, suddenly reveals itself as an '''epoch''' of fading and decay.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|last=Kołakowski|first=Leszek|authorlink=Leszek Kołakowski|title=w:Main Currents of Marxism|volume=II: The Golden Age|origlang=pl|original=Główne nurty marksizmu: rozwój|translator=P[aul] S[teven] Falla|origyear=1976|publisher=w:Clarendon Press|location=w:Oxford|isbn=9780198245698|oclc=961252281|page=442|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/maincurrentsofma00lesz/page/442|passage=We thus reach a position of extreme relativism: different 'truths' may be useful in different historical situations, and it is quite possible that any truth is valid only for a particular '''epoch''' or social class.}}
# A [[notable]] [[event]] which marks the [[beginning#Noun|beginning]] of such a period.
# {{lb|en|chronology|astronomy|computing}} A specific [[instant]] in time, chosen as the [[point of reference]] or [[zero]] value of a system that involves identifying instants of time.
#: {{cot|en|datum<q:cartography, engineering>}}
#: {{ux|en|UNIX '''epoch'''; J2000 '''epoch'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Carl D. Murray; Stanley F. Dermott|title=Solar System Dynamics|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=9781139936156|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=I_8LBAAAQBAJ&pg=PT93&dq=J2000+%22epoch%22&hl=&cd=12&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=J2000%20%22epoch%22&f=false|text=Appendix A gives formulae for the calculation of the orbital elements of the planets at any time referred to the mean ecliptic and equinox of the '''epoch''' of noon on 1st January 2000; this is called the J2000 '''epoch'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Preston Miller; Chapin Bryce|title=Learning Python for Forensics|publisher=Packt Publishing Ltd|isbn=9781783285242|page=281|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ggNwDQAAQBAJ&pg=PA281&dq=%22epoch%22+1970&hl=&cd=2&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22epoch%22%201970&f=false|text=There are two major '''epoch''' times associated with most timestamps: <code>1970-01-01 00:00:00</code> and <code>1601-01-01 00:00:00</code>. The first, starting in 1970, is traditionally referred to as POSIX time as it is a common timestamp in UNIX and UNIX-like systems.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=Tomasz Lelek; Jon Skeet|title=Software Mistakes and Tradeoffs: How to Make Good Programming Decisions|publisher=Simon and Schuster|isbn=9781617299209|page=155|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=S8JrEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA155&dq=%22proleptic%22+.NET&hl=&cd=2&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22proleptic%22%20.NET&f=false|text=The .NET '''epoch''' is midnight at the start of January 1st, AD 1, although that's AD 1 in a proleptic Gregorian calendar, which refers to even ''more'' complexity we haven't talked about yet.}}
# {{senseid|en|geology}}{{lb|en|geology}} A [[geochronologic]] [[unit]] of [[hundred]]s of [[thousand]]s to [[million]]s of [[year]]s; a [[subdivision]] of a [[period]], and [[subdivided]] into [[age]]s (or sometimes [[subepoch]]s).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1881|title=Island Life|author=w:Alfred Russel Wallace|chapter=VIII|chapterurl={{fullurl:s:en:Island Life/VIII}}|passage=Now during the time of the glacial '''epoch''' the greatest distance of the sun in winter was 98¼ millions of miles, whereas it is now, in winter, only 91½ millions of miles, the mean distance being taken as 93 million miles.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Donald Worster|title=A Drier and Hotter Future|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/a-drier-and-hotter-future|magazine=w:American Scientist|month=January|year=2012|volume=100|issue=1|page=70|passage=Phoenix and Lubbock are both caught in severe drought, and it is going to get much worse. We may see many such [dust] storms in the decades ahead, along with species extinctions, radical disturbance of ecosystems, and intensified social conflict over land and water. Welcome to the Anthropocene, the '''epoch''' when humans have become a major geological and climatic force.|accessdate=12 May 2013|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120126223603/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/a-drier-and-hotter-future|archivedate=26 January 2012}}
# {{lb|en|ML}} One complete [[presentation]] of the [[training#Noun|training]] [[data]] set to an [[iterative]] [[machine learning]] [[algorithm]].
