# {{lb|en|transitive}} To provide evidence for, or suggest the truth of.
#: {{ux|en|She was furious, as '''evidenced''' by her slamming the door.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1941|month=May|title=Notes and News: William Stroudley|journal=Railway Magazine|page=234|text=That he ''was'' a great locomotive engineer, it would be foolish to deny or even to qualify; that he was also extremely pig-headed is fairly '''evidenced''' by David Joy, who in his 'Diaries' said that Stroudley always wanted his way 'to the last nut and bolt.'}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=October|author=Brian Haresnape|title=Focus on B.R. passenger stations|journal=Modern Railways|pages=250–251|text=Elegant brick and stone buildings, with iron and glass canopies and decorative wooden scalloping and fencing—all '''evidencing''' care on the part of the architect to produce a pleasing, well-planned building—were submerged beneath a profusion of ill-conceived additions and camouflaged by vulgar paint schemes; and the original conception was lost.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 April 6|author=Conrad Landin|title=ScotRail in the public eye...|journal=RAIL|issue=954|page=39|text="And I think we can do better, and we have to do better, because we need to '''evidence''' why public ownership of the railways is going to work for the people who use it.}}

