# {{lb|en|military|also|figuratively}}
## A [[soldier#Noun|soldier]] [[engage]]d in [[design#Verb|designing]] or [[construct#Verb|constructing]] [[military]] [[works#Noun|works]] for [[attack#Noun|attack]] or [[defence#Noun|defence]], or other [[engineering#Noun|engineering]] works.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet Q1-2|version=Q2|act=III|scene=iv|sig=&#91;I4&#93;|verso=1|page=70|passage=For tis the ſport to haue the '''enginer''' / Hoiſt with his ovvne [[petard|petar]], an't ſhall goe hard / But I vvill delue one yard belovve their mines, / And blovve them at the Moone: {{...}}|translation=For it's amusing to have the '''engineer''' / Hoisted into the sky with his own explosive, and if I'm lucky / I will dig one yard below their mines, / And blow them towards the Moon: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Purchas Pilgrimes|author=Edmund Scot|part=1|chapter=A Discourse of ''Iaua'', and of the First ''English'' Factorie there, with Diuers ''Indian'', ''English'', and ''Dutch'' Occurrents,{{nb...|Written by Master Edmund Scot, Contayning a Historie of Things Done from the Eleuenth of Februarie, 1602. till the Sixt of October, 1605. Abbreuiated.}}|page=173|section=IIII|passage=Novv he began another Trade, and became an '''Ingenor''', hauing got eight Fire-brands of hell more to him, onely of purpoſe to ſet our houſe a fire.}}
##* {{RQ:Drayton Battle of Agincourt|page=12|passage=Cannons vpon their Carriage mounted are, / VVhole Battery ''Fraunce'' muſt feele vpon her VValls, / The '''Engineer''' prouiding the Petar, / To breake the ſtrong Percullice, and the Balls / Of VVild fire deuis'd to throvv from farre, / To burne to ground their Pallaces and Halls: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Burke Works|volume=XV|title=Hastings 1|pages=63–64|pageref=63|passage=But your Lordships must have heard with astonishment, that, upon points of law, relative to the tenure of lands, instead of producing any law document or authority on the usages and local customs of the country, he has referred to officers in the army, colonels of artillery and '''engineers''', to young gentlemen just come from school, not above three or four years in the country.}}
##* {{RQ:Kingsley Hereward the Wake|volume=I|page=341|passage=And she began praising [[w:Hereward the Wake|Hereward]]'s valour, his fame, his eloquence, his skill as a general and '''engineer'''; and when he suggested, smiling, that he was an exile and an outlaw, she insisted he was all the fitter from that very fact.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A soldier [[in charge of]] [[operate|operating]] a [[weapon#Noun|weapon]]; an [[artilleryman]], a [[gunner]].
##* {{RQ:Heywood Edward 4|sig=N|page=96|passage=This is hard welcome, but it was not you, / At whom the fatal '''enginer''' did ayme, / My breaſt the levell was, though you the marke, / In which conſpiracie anſwere me Duke, / Is not thy ſoule as guiltie as the Earles?}}
##* {{RQ:Herbert Temple|chapter=The Church-porch|page=9|passage=Wit's an unruly engine, wildly ſtriking / Sometimes a friend, ſometimes the '''engineer'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Addison Freeholder|issue=19|date=24 February 1716|page=426|passage=An Author who points his [[satire|ſatyr]] at a great man, is to be looked upon in the ſame view with the '''engineer''' who ſignalized himſelf by this ungenerous practice.}}
##* {{RQ:Whitman Leaves of Grass|edition=1st|page=v|column=1|passage=In war he [the poet] is the most deadly force of the war. Who recruits him recruits horse and foot&nbsp;… he fetches parks of artillery the best that '''engineer''' ever knew.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}}
#: {{syn|en|hostler}}
## A person professionally engaged in the [[technical#Adjective|technical]] [[design#Noun|design]] and [[construction]] of [[large-scale]] [[private#Adjective|private]] and [[public works]] such as [[bridge#Noun|bridges]], [[building#Noun|buildings]], [[harbour#Noun|harbours]], [[railway]]s, [[road#Noun|roads]], etc.; a [[civil engineer]].
##* {{RQ:Suetonius Holland Caesars|section=17|page=249|passage=[T]o an '''Enginer''' alſo, vvho promiſed to bring into the Capitoll huge ''Columnes'' vvith ſmall charges, hee gave for his deviſe no meane revvard; and releaſed him his labour in performing that vvorke, ſaying vvithall by vvay of preface, ''That he ſhould ſuffer him to feed the poore commons''.}}
## {{senseid|en|person certified in CivEng, MechEng, ElecEng, IndusEng, SysEng, or similar}} Originally, a person engaged in designing, constructing, or [[maintain]]ing [[engine#Noun|engines]] or [[machinery]]; now {{lb|en|more generally}}, a person [[qualified#Adjective|qualified]] or [[professionally]] [[engaged#Adjective|engaged]] in any [[branch#Noun|branch]] of engineering, or [[study#Verb|studying]] to do so.
##* {{RQ:Florio Worlde of Wordes|entry=Macanopoietico|page=209|column=3|passage=Macanopoietico, an '''inginer''', an engine-maker.}}
##* {{RQ:Middleton Works|volume=VII|play=The Triumphs of Integrity|page=391|passage=[N]ear St. Laurence-Lane his lordship receives an entertainment from an unparalleled masterpiece of art, called the Crystal Sanctuary, styled by the name of the Temple of Integrity, {{...}} and more to express the invention and the art of the '''engineer''', as also for motion, variety, and the content of the spectators, this Crystal Temple is made to open in many parts, at fit and convenient times, and upon occasion of the speech; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Atlantic|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20151106025821/https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2015/11/programmers-should-not-call-themselves-engineers/414271/|title=Programmers: Stop Calling Yourselves Engineers|date=2015-11-05|author=w:Ian Bogost|passage=Somehow, everybody who isn’t in sales, marketing, or design became an '''engineer'''. “We’re hiring '''engineers''',” read startup websites, which could mean anything from Javascript{{sic}} programmers to roboticists.}}
## A person [[train#Verb|trained]] to operate an engine.
### {{lb|en|chiefly|historical}} A person who operates a [[steam engine]]; specifically {{lb|en|nautical}}, a person [[employ#Verb|employed]] to operate the steam engine in the [[engine room]] of a [[ship#Noun|ship]].
###: {{syn|en|engineman}}
###* {{RQ:Emerson English Traits|chapter=Wealth|pages=170–171|pageref=171|passage=The machinery [the steam engine] has proved, like the balloon, unmanageable, and flies away with the aeronaut. Steam, from the first, hissed and screamed to warn him; it was dreadful with its explosion, and crushed the '''engineer'''. The machinist has wrought and watched, '''engineers''' and firemen without number have been sacrificed in learning to tame and guide the monster.}}
###* {{RQ:Whitman Leaves of Grass|poem=Song of the Answerer|part=1|page=136|passage=The '''engineer''', the deck-hand on the great lakes, or on the Mississippi or St. Lawrence or Sacramento, or Hudson or Paumanok sound, claims him.}}
###* {{RQ:Conrad Typhoon|chapter=IV|page=226|column=2|passage=One of the stokers was disabled, the others had given in, the second '''engineer''' and the donkey-man were firing-up. The third '''engineer''' was standing by the steam-valve. The engines were being tended by hand.}}
### {{lb|en|US|firefighting}} A person who [[drive#Verb|drives]] or operates a [[fire engine]] (firefighting apparatus).
### {{senseid|en|train driver, locomotive driver}} {{lb|en|chiefly|US|rail transport}} A person who drives or operates a [[locomotive#Noun|locomotive]]; a [[train#Noun|train]] [[driver]].
###: {{hyper|en|footplateman}}
###: {{cot|en|fireman<id:an assistant on any locomotive>}}
## ''Preceded by a qualifying word'': a person who [[use#Verb|uses]] [[ability|abilities]] or [[knowledge]] to [[manipulate]] [[event#Noun|events]] or people.
##: {{ux|en|a political '''engineer'''}}
##* {{RQ:Defoe System of Magick|chapter=Of the Present Pretences of the Magicians: How They Defend Themselves; and Some Examples of Their Practice|page=319|passage=Now that I may not ſeem to paſs my Cenſure raſhly, I deſire that my more intelligent Readers will pleaſe to reduce the following things into Meaning, if they can, and favour us with the Interpretation; being ſome particular Account of the Life of this famous, religious '''Ingineer''', for I know not what elſe to call him, and the Titles of ſome of his Books.}}
## {{lb|en|often|derogatory}} A person who [[formulate]]s [[plot#Noun|plots]] or [[scheme#Noun|schemes]]; a [[plotter]], a [[schemer]].
##* {{RQ:Harvey Pierces Supererogation|year=1870|page=10|passage=But the trimme ſilke-worme I looked for (as it were in a proper contempt of common fineneſſe) prooveth but a ſilly glow-woorme, and the dreadfull '''enginer''' of phraſes, in steede of thunderboltes, ſhooteth nothing but dogboltes and catboltes, and the homelieſt boltes of rude folly: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Jonson Sejanus|sig=B|page=16|passage=No ''Silius'', vve are no good '''Inginers'''; / VVe vvant the fine Artes, & their thriuing vſe / Should make vs grac'd, or fauour'd of the Times: / {{...}} / VVe burne vvith no black ſecrets, vvhich can make / Vs deare to the pale Authors; or liue fear'd / Of their ſtill vvaking iealoſies, to raiſe / Our ſelues a Fortune, by ſubuerting theirs.}}
##* {{RQ:London People of the Abyss|chapter=Coronation Day|page=144|passage=[T]he fighting men of England, masters of destruction, '''engineers''' of death!}}
