===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|ambassy<ety:bor<frm:ambassee<ety:inh<fro:ambascee<ety:der<roa-oit:ambasciata>>>>>>|tree=1}}
Modern variant of obsolete {{m|en|ambassy}}, from {{bor|en|frm|ambassee||mission, embassy}}, from {{der|en|fro|ambascee}} (also {{m|fro|enbassee||message for a high official, official mission}}) from {{der|en|roa-oit|ambasciata}}, from {{der|en|pro|ambaissada||embassy}}, derived from {{m|pro|ambaissa||message}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|ambactia||service rendered}} (attested also as {{m|la|ambascia}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*ambahtiją||service}}, {{m|gem-pro|*ambahtaz||follower, servant}}, from {{der|en|cel-gau|ambaxtos|t=dependant, vassal|lit=one who is sent around}}, from {{der|en|cel-pro|*ambaxtos||servant}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*[[h₂m̥bʰi]]-[[h₂eǵ-]]|t=drive around}}; compare {{cog|la|ambactus}}, {{cog|sga|amus}}, {{m|sga|amsach||mercenary, servant}}, {{cog|cy|amaeth||tenant farm}}). {{doublet|en|ambassade}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛmbəsi/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-embassy.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The function or duty of an [[ambassador]].
#* {{RQ:Temple Works|volume=I|chapter=I|page=379|passage=The King told me, {{...}} That I ſhould have the Character of Ambaſſador Extraordinary, and the ſame Allovvance I ſhou'd have had in ''Spain'': Upon this Offer I made no Demurr, but immediately accepted it, and ſo my '''Ambaſſy''' vvas declar'd in ''May'' 1674.}}
# An organization or group of officials who permanently represent a sovereign state in a second sovereign state or with respect to an international organization such as the United Nations.
#: {{ux|en|The American '''embassy''' to France is located in Paris.}}
# A temporary mission representing a sovereign state.
#: {{ux|en|The Japanese '''embassy''' to the United States traveled to Washington, D.C., where it was received by {{w|James Buchanan}}, before continuing on to New York and then returning to Japan, making several stops on the way.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|I|i|text=Then go we in to know his '''embassy''';<br>Which I could with a ready guess declare<br>Before the Frenchman speak a word of it.}}
# The official residence of such a group, or of an [[ambassador]].
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[written]] [[account]] as to an embassy.
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=[[s:A Full and True Relation of the Great and Wonderful Revolution That Hapned Lately in the Kingdom of Siam in the East-Indies/Part 1|A Relation of the Late Great Revolution in Siam, and the Driving Out of the French]]|year=1690|page=1|location=London|publisher=Randal Taylor|text=By the various Relations, '''Embaſſies''' and Voyages of Siam that have been publiſht within theſe laſt Four Years {{...}}|title=A Full and True Relation of the Great and Wonderful Revolution That Hapned Lately in the Kingdom of Siam in the East-Indies}}

====Usage notes====
Today the term '''embassy''' generally refers to a ''permanent'' organization, housed in a permanent building or offices, maintaining ties between nations. In the past, this was more often a ''temporary'' mission (as in {{w|Japanese Embassy to the United States (1860)}}) – the delegation would return home following meetings with foreign officials – and this is reflected in some traditional usages and related diplomatic terms, such as {{w|Head of Mission}}.

